The code statements that lead to the bug are:
head = *headbuf;
if (unlikely(!tipc_msg_validate(&head)))
    goto err;
err:
    kfree_skb(*headbuf);
Reason:
The local variable `head` is a copy of `*headbuf`. If `tipc_msg_validate()` reallocates the skb (freeing the old one and updating `head`), the error path frees `*headbuf` which still points to the already-freed original skb, causing a double-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (unlikely(!tipc_msg_validate(&head))) {
    if (head != *headbuf)
        *headbuf = head;
    goto err;
}
Reason:
After `tipc_msg_validate()`, if `head` has been reallocated to a new skb (i.e., `head != *headbuf`), `*headbuf` is updated to point to the new skb. This ensures the error path frees the correct skb, preventing a double-free of the old skb.

